# Release Notes

Release notes live in `CHANGELOG.md` at the repo root. The format follows
[Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/) and the project uses
[Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/).

## Golden rules

- **Short and to the point.** One line per change. No filler.
- **Functional, not technical** — except in the Technical section.
- **Write for the reader of that section.** A public entry is read by end users;
  an admin entry by hub operators; a technical entry by developers.
- **Latest version first.** Newest release at the top, under `Unreleased`.
- **Omit empty sections.** Only include a section when it has entries.

## Structure

Keep a top `## [Unreleased]` section; move it into a dated version at release time.

Each version is split into three fixed sections, always in this order:

```markdown
## [Unreleased]

### Public features
<!-- End-user facing changes: the simulation, public pages, e-mails users receive. -->
- ...

### Admin features
<!-- Admin zone changes: entities, configuration, exports/imports operators use. -->
- ...

### Technical
<!-- Developer-facing changes: noteworthy refactors, dependencies, breaking APIs. -->
- ...
```

## Writing each section

### Public features & Admin features (functional)

- Describe **what changed for the person using it**, not how it was built.
- No code, file paths, function names, or table names.
- Prefix with a Keep a Changelog verb when it adds clarity: `Added`, `Changed`,
  `Fixed`, `Removed`, `Deprecated`, `Security`.

Good:

- `Changed: cars with a too-high depreciation cost are now rejected automatically.`
- `Added: admins can set a maximum accepted price per hub.`

Avoid:

- `Refactored runSimulationEngine to extract simulationCarIsCategoryBCandidate.`
  (technical — belongs in Technical, and too detailed)

### Technical

- Here detail is welcome: noteworthy refactors, new dependencies, breaking
  internal changes.
- Call out **breaking changes** explicitly.
- **Do not** add entries that only announce a routine schema/Prisma migration
  (e.g. “migration for nullable `foo` on bar”). If the change already has a
  Public or Admin entry, that is enough. Mention a migration only when it is
  itself noteworthy: breaking, destructive, requires a data backfill, or
  needs operator action beyond a normal deploy.

## Versioning

- `MAJOR` — breaking change for users or integrators.
- `MINOR` — new backward-compatible feature.
- `PATCH` — backward-compatible bug fix.

## When to update

Add an entry under `## [Unreleased]` in the same change that ships a
user-, admin-, or developer-visible change. Derive the entry from the change
itself, not from the git log.
